        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "Innate defense begins before an immune cell attacks anything. Skin and mucosal epithelium form a physical and chemical barrier. When that barrier is breached, tissue-resident sentinel cells are already nearby. Macrophages, dendritic cells, and other cells sample their environment for signs of microbes and tissue damage. Their job is not to identify one exact strain from memory. It is to recognize recurring molecular patterns and rapidly decide whether the local tissue needs help. That speed is the defining strength of innate immunity."
        },
        "model": "edge-tts-7.2.8",
        "parameters": {},
        "schemaVersion": 1
