        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "Pattern-recognition receptors detect conserved features associated with pathogens or damaged cells. Binding activates signaling pathways inside the sentinel cell. The result is a change of state: from quiet monitoring to alarm. The cell releases cytokines and chemokines that alter nearby blood vessels and guide circulating leukocytes toward the tissue. Notice the logic: recognition is local, but the response recruits system-wide resources. The alarm must be strong enough to mobilize help without becoming uncontrolled."
        },
        "model": "edge-tts-7.2.8",
