        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "Inflammation changes blood flow, vascular permeability, and cell adhesion near the affected tissue. Plasma proteins can enter the site, and vessel walls display signals that slow passing leukocytes. These changes explain redness, warmth, swelling, and pain. They are not random side effects; they create access and concentration where defense is needed. Yet the same mechanisms can damage healthy tissue if excessive or prolonged. The response is therefore a controlled trade-off between rapid containment and collateral harm."
        },
